{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for exact 412a334bdb6d6a95afdc604992cba6b8d52e9d8c (tree617f0e40d4275dcec32119d097cbc63b5242de69), both Sources examined, primary first.\n\nArchitecture: successor scopes are a reasonable choice, and D5-5 is satisfied by an immutable fact read. Four gaps prevent approval:\n1. P1 — Reconcile the terminal journal with late deliveries (note240,397–400,469–485). The final entry is defined as the predecessor's last entry, and the floor rule promises no later entries. Yet every later delivery is recorded refused, as D1-7 requires. The contract3427 explicitly makes a refused delivery an entry. Therefore one ordinary late delivery invalidates both terminal-history claims; repeated late deliveries keep extending the supposedly finished journal. Choose one coherent rule and state its effects on delivery results/retries, replay and archive/floor bounds. Add a late-delivery witness. An immutable cutoff export with a continuing refusal journal is one possible design, but then it is not the last journal entry.\n2. P1 — Design the carried-item identity rule (note241–242,271–277,330–332,567–569). Current Item.id is the seq of its opening entry (contract/read.ts45; scope contract3276–3283), with at most one item per entry. fold.ts108–115 rejects every open whose ID differs from entry.seq. There are no per-type next-ID counters to export. A genesis at0 cannot open the fixture's several carried items at their old IDs; a new scope's ordinary entry at an inherited ID would also collide. Define the new import/open representation, future ID allocation and opening-fact provenance, including what local item/fact references mean after carry-forward. List the affected contract/derive/fold/replay/read statements in section15. A witness must carry several nonzero IDs and then open a fresh item without collision.\n3. P1 — Bind the authorized cutoff to exactly one successor (note263–290,457–461,477–479). The final entry names only the successor definition and mapping. The three link checks require neither equality of the successor creator with the predecessor's authorized creator nor a canonical complete successor seed. Existing Seed also has kind, cause and ordinal (scope.ts44–51), which the proposed computation omits; normal creation binds these to a verified creator send (genesis.ts139–145). Two different seeds/creators can cite the same final fact with the same definition and mapping and satisfy the three stated link checks. Those checks do not establish the M3 promise of one exerciser. Specify the full seed derivation/creation proof, bind its creator and all identity fields, and reject a second distinct successor for the same cutoff without introducing a self-hash cycle. Add negative witnesses for a different creator and a second seed/cause/ordinal, plus crash/retry recovery of the intended identity.\n4. P2 — Narrow or complete the atomicity guarantee (note283–290). Equality of the mapping check establishes equality of mapping outcomes. It does not by itself prove that a successor genesis will be accepted. Today's genesis additionally checks creation proof/fields/facts, derives the definition's genesis guards/effects/sends and can write a terminal refused genesis (genesis.ts149–211). State whether the new succession genesis replaces that ordinary path or runs it, and which checks are prevalidated/reserved before cutoff. A permanently refused genesis must not strand an already retired application under the claimed fixed-seed retry guarantee. A failed-mapping witness alone does not distinguish this. Separate temporary host/dependency unavailability from permanent refusal and include a post-mapping genesis-refusal witness or a construction that makes it impossible.\n\nSecurity: activation/admin/creator checks, stale-act refusal and keeping unknown outside work in the predecessor are sound directions. Finding3 is mandatory to establish nonduplication of carried authority; this is a design proof gap, not a demonstrated current implementation exploit. Peer succession remains explicitly disabled until S5. Retirement does not remove relation copies or free a fan-in slot: the state.ts419–424 lifetime count supports section13's distinction from retiring a room item.\n\nSimplification: keep pin-for-life and a data mapping; do not introduce same-scope redefinition to solve these findings. Resolve the terminal-history and item-identity rules before slices rely on them. Document the exact successor construction once and reuse it for forward reads and replay.\n\nEvidence: complete primary note and roadmap delta read against immutable Mainc40492ee; cited spike rules/invariant, attach implementation, initialization gap, Sale budget, locality results and ordered export protocol inspected as DATA at9d738e2e. The spike's report supports only its executed fixtures, not general migration. 26 pinnedBy/pinnedOf call lines (28 matching lines minus2 declarations) and34 source files reading seed.definition agree with the note. Some line citations need correction: section10 cites history-note52–69, which lists platform pins/cohorts, not application package retention; section2's partition57–61 defines sealing, while partition-sealed refusal is at275/424. Correct these while revising.\n\nValidation: documentation only; exactly two changed paths and no source/test changes. Immutable git diff --check passed. Under docs/testing.md's document-only rule no gate was repeated. Baseline treeef2c2b54e24f470551d360b7bcc50221410dd9cb; candidate tree617f0e40d4275dcec32119d097cbc63b5242de69. No candidate/helper/compiler/test/runtime execution, source edits, network, landing or deployment by checker.\n\nFrozen checker DATA evidence: /private/tmp/checker-succession-draft1-PtK9qV5D; manifest263B0444 SHA2561967ec589ff4aa7aea5af6ba3f24a83698e797f2c97a8367f2eff4f2c06bf72a. Never execute/import/edit/chmod.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a086a5051119d08dbb32ab1705376a2a96e